    body: "<p style='color:#6a4b8f;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:18px'>By Khyati Srivastava</p><p>Behind the cottage of twins Mira and Dov grew a peculiar garden. There, flowers did everything in reverse. They opened in full bloom, then slowly folded into buds, then shrank into seeds.</p><p>The twins did not understand it until a traveling beekeeper explained. This garden does not grow forward, he said. It grows toward beginnings. Whatever you give it, it returns to its purest form.</p><p>Mira wanted to test this. She planted a wilting friendship, a quarrel she had had with Dov that had grown thorny and bitter. She buried the memory like a seed.</p><p>For days the garden worked. The angry bloom curled inward, petal by petal, shedding its sharp edges, until all that remained in the soil was one small, bright seed.</p><p>When Mira lifted it, she felt only the warm beginning of their friendship, before the harsh words, when they had simply loved being together.</p><p>She showed it to Dov, and he understood at once. They planted the little seed side by side, and this time they let it grow forward, into something gentler than before.</p><p>The garden taught them that even the things that go wrong can be returned to where they started, and grown again with kinder hands.</p>",
