    created_at: "2026-06-09T12:00:00Z",
    body: "<p style='color:#6a4b8f;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:18px'>By Khyati Srivastava</p><p>Long ago, the stars did not hang themselves in the sky. Each night a great sky-whale named Lumen swam up from the dark ocean, her back glittering with starlight, and scattered the stars into place with a flick of her tail.</p><p>The children of the coast loved her. They left her gifts of seaweed and sang to her as she rose.</p><p>But Lumen was growing old. One night she could not lift herself from the waves. The sky stayed black and empty, and the world below grew frightened in the dark.</p><p>A fisher girl named Tovi rowed out to the struggling whale. You carried our light for so long, she said. Now let us carry yours.</p><p>Tovi called every boat in the village. They lit their lanterns and tied them together, hundreds of little flames, and lifted them high on their masts. The lights bobbed across the water like stars come down to sea.</p><p>Lumen saw the glow and found her strength again. Slowly she rose, and as she climbed, the villagers released their lanterns to float skyward, helping her hang the stars one last beautiful time.</p><p>After that night, Lumen rested in the deep. But the villagers kept lifting their lanterns, and that is why, they say, the sky and the sea share the same lights.</p>",
