    body: "<p style='color:#6a4b8f;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:18px'>By Khyati Srivastava</p><p>Bertie was a scribe who loved to stretch the truth. He wrote letters for the villagers and added flourishes that were not quite real, prettier weather, grander feasts, braver deeds.</p><p>One day a wandering merchant traded him a dusty old inkwell. It glowed faintly blue, and from then on, whatever Bertie tried to write came out as the plain honest truth.</p><p>When he wrote that the baker's bread was the finest in the land, the pen scratched out, The bread was a little burnt but made with love. Bertie was horrified.</p><p>At first the honest letters caused a stir. But strange things happened. The baker, reading the truth, laughed and fixed his oven. A shy farmer, whose letter said simply, He misses his daughter very much, finally wrote to her and she came home.</p><p>People began to trust Bertie's letters more than ever, because they knew the words were real. His honest letters mended quarrels and rekindled friendships across the whole village.</p><p>Bertie realized he had never needed his fancy fibs at all. The truth, written kindly, was the most beautiful thing he could put on a page.</p><p>He kept that inkwell for the rest of his life, and never wrote another false word again.</p>",
