    body: "<p style='color:#6a4b8f;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:18px'>By Khyati Srivastava</p><p>At the edge of the valley stood a mountain named Cael who was terribly lonely. Mountains cannot move, and Cael had stood in the same spot since the beginning of time, watching clouds drift to faraway places he would never see.</p><p>A young shepherd named Rue often rested on his slopes. One evening she heard a deep, sad rumble and realized the mountain was sighing. When she asked why, Cael spoke in a voice like distant thunder. I wish to see the world, but I can never leave.</p><p>Rue thought hard. The next spring, before her travels, she promised the mountain something.</p><p>All through her journeys, Rue gathered things. A sea shell from the coast, a pressed desert flower, a vial of glittering river sand, a feather from a tropical bird. Each evening of her trip she described aloud where she was, knowing the wind would carry her words home.</p><p>When she returned, she planted her treasures along Cael's slopes and told him every story, of oceans and cities and snow.</p><p>Travelers began to climb the mountain just to see Rue's little wonders and hear the tales tied to them. Cael was never lonely again, for now the whole world came to visit him, one curious climber at a time.</p>",
