    excerpt: "A shy girl's shadow secretly dreams of dancing, and one moonlit night it finally asks for help.",
    created_at: "2026-06-07T06:00:00Z",
    body: "<p style='color:#6a4b8f;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:18px'>By Khyati Srivastava</p><p>Wren was too shy to dance. At every festival she stood at the back while others twirled, her cheeks burning, her feet still.</p><p>One moonlit night, Wren noticed her shadow doing something impossible. While she sat perfectly still, her shadow swayed and spun across the wall. Startled, Wren whispered, How are you doing that?</p><p>The shadow paused, then wrote on the wall with a shadowy finger, I have always wanted to dance. But I can only move when you do.</p><p>Wren felt a pang of guilt. Her own fear had kept her shadow frozen too, all these years.</p><p>So, in the safety of her empty room, Wren took one small step. Her shadow leapt with joy. She took another, and another, and soon the two of them were spinning together, real girl and dancing shadow, laughing in the moonlight.</p><p>Night after night they practiced, until Wren forgot to be afraid. By the time the spring festival came, her feet knew the steps by heart.</p><p>This time Wren did not stand at the back. She stepped into the circle and danced, and her shadow danced beside her, finally free.</p><p>The other villagers cheered, but Wren smiled only at the shape on the ground who had believed in her first.</p>",
