    body: "<p style='color:#6a4b8f;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:18px'>By Khyati Srivastava</p><p>Beyond the meadow stood a bridge of pure glass that led to the Vale of Wishes. It was said the bridge held only those who spoke the truth, and crumbled beneath anyone who told a lie.</p><p>Many proud knights and merchants tried to cross. They boasted of brave deeds and grand riches, and one by one the glass cracked beneath their feet and tumbled them gently into the soft grass below.</p><p>A goose girl named Hetty watched them fail. She was poor and plain, and she only wished to find a cure for her ailing grandmother in the Vale.</p><p>Trembling, she stepped onto the glass. A magic voice asked, Who are you?</p><p>I am no one special, Hetty answered honestly. I have no gold and no great deeds. I am only a girl who loves her grandmother and is rather afraid right now.</p><p>The glass beneath her did not crack. It held firm, clear and strong, all the way across, for every word she spoke was true.</p><p>In the Vale she found the healing herb she needed. When she returned and cured her grandmother, the villagers asked how she had succeeded where mighty knights had failed.</p><p>Hetty smiled and said, I simply told the truth. It turns out that is the strongest thing to stand on.</p>",
