    created_at: "2026-06-03T12:00:00Z",
    body: "<p style='color:#6a4b8f;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:18px'>By Khyati Srivastava</p><p>Old Hass the cobbler mended shoes in a crooked little shop at the end of Lantern Lane. He was poor in coin but rich in patience, and he never turned anyone away.</p><p>One snowy evening, a sad woman came in, not with shoes, but with tears. My dream has fallen apart, she said. I once wished to be a singer, but life wore the wish down to nothing.</p><p>Hass did not know what to say, so he did what he always did. He took out his needle and thread and, on a whim, began to stitch the air where her wish should be.</p><p>To his amazement, golden threads appeared between his fingers, and the woman's old dream slowly came back together, patched and glowing.</p><p>The next morning she sang in the square, and her voice rang so true that the whole town gathered to listen.</p><p>Word spread, and soon people came to Lantern Lane not just with broken shoes but with broken wishes, a faded hope, a forgotten goal, a courage worn thin.</p><p>Hass mended them all, one careful stitch at a time, asking nothing in return but a smile.</p><p>And though he never grew rich, the cobbler who mended wishes was the happiest man in town, for his days were full of dreams set right.</p>",
