    body: "<p style='color:#6a4b8f;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:18px'>By Khyati Srivastava</p><p>The Queen of the North ruled a palace of ice where nothing ever grew. She was not cruel, only lonely, and her loneliness made the whole kingdom cold. Frost roses bloomed in her garden, beautiful but freezing to the touch.</p><p>A village child named Sefa wandered into the palace one day, lost in a snowstorm. Instead of fearing the queen, she offered her a warm woolen mitten she had knitted.</p><p>No one had ever given the queen a gift before. She took the small mitten and felt, for the first time in years, a tiny spark of warmth in her chest.</p><p>Sefa stayed and kept the queen company, telling her about her family and friends, about laughter and shared meals and bedtime songs.</p><p>With each story, the spark in the queen's heart grew. And as it grew, something astonishing happened in the garden. One frost rose turned soft and red, then another, until the icy blooms became real, fragrant roses that no longer froze the hand.</p><p>The warmth spread from the palace across the whole kingdom. Snow melted into springs, and meadows woke from their long sleep.</p><p>The queen was lonely no more. She opened her gates to all, and travelers came from far away to see the warmest garden in the once-frozen North, tended by a queen and the brave little girl who thawed her heart.</p>",
