    created_at: "2026-12-12T17:00:00Z",
    body: "<p style='color:#6a4b8f;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:18px'>By Khyati Srivastava</p><p>Priya's alarm clock had stopped functioning properly months earlier, according to every electronics repair shop she'd consulted, its internal mechanism genuinely, verifiably broken beyond reliable operation. It nonetheless continued, inexplicably, to wake her precisely at moments that, she gradually realized, consistently prevented her from encountering specific situations that would have genuinely threatened her hard-won sobriety during a particularly vulnerable early recovery period.</p><p>She tracked the pattern deliberately over several months, noting each unexpected, unexplained wake-up against her actual daily schedule, and found the timing correlated, with unsettling precision, against moments she'd otherwise have encountered old triggering environments or acquaintances connected to her previous struggles.</p><p>Her sponsor, hearing about the strange pattern during a support meeting, offered a perspective grounded more in genuine gratitude than rational explanation. \"I don't think it matters whether there's a technical explanation or something else entirely happening,\" she said. \"What matters is that something's been protecting your recovery, consistently, at exactly the moments you needed it most. Take the help, however it's actually arriving.\"</p><p>Priya kept the technically broken clock rather than replacing it with a reliably functioning one, understanding, gradually, that whatever inexplicable mechanism kept it running had become genuinely important to her ongoing recovery in ways she'd stopped needing to fully understand. \"I don't know if it's actually protecting me or if I'm just noticing coincidences more carefully now that I'm paying closer attention to my own patterns,\" she told her sponsor. \"Either way, it's never once been wrong when it mattered most.\"</p>",
