    body: "<p style='color:#6a4b8f;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:18px'>By Khyati Srivastava</p><p>Priya inherited the antique mirror from her aunt's estate, appreciating its ornate frame without initially discovering its unsettling quality: on certain evenings, her reflection briefly, unmistakably shifted into a different version of herself — recognizable but subtly older, considerably more confident, clearly having persisted through the painting career she'd genuinely abandoned in her twenties rather than pursuing it seriously.</p><p>The alternate reflection never spoke or interacted directly, simply appeared, briefly, going about ordinary tasks in what appeared to be a genuinely different life — sketching in a studio, examining a gallery show clearly featuring her own work, moments Priya's actual life had never included after she'd abandoned art for a more financially secure career path.</p><p>She found the experience genuinely unsettling initially, a constant, visual reminder of a path not taken, until she noticed something important during a particularly clear reflection: the alternate version, while clearly successful in her art career, appeared considerably more exhausted and financially strained than Priya's own actual, stable life had allowed her family.</p><p>She understood, gradually, that the mirror wasn't simply showing a superior life she'd tragically missed, but an honest, complete alternate reality with its own genuine costs and trade-offs entirely absent from her own comfortable assumptions about what she'd given up. She stopped feeling regretful watching the reflections after that, understanding, finally, that both versions of her life held genuine value and genuine sacrifice in roughly equal measure. \"I don't need the mirror anymore to know I made a real choice, not just a loss,\" she told a friend, having gradually made peace with whatever strange gift the old mirror had actually offered her.</p>",
