    body: "<p style='color:#6a4b8f;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:18px'>By Khyati Srivastava</p><p>The anger burned hot at first, consuming everything —<br>every thought, every conversation, every quiet hour.<br>I let it burn. I didn't rush to smother it.<br>I trusted, somehow, in its genuine, necessary power.</p><p>It burned for weeks. Then, slowly, it burned lower.<br>Not gone entirely. Just no longer all I had.<br>Underneath the ash, I found something softer waiting —<br>genuine grief, honest hurt, considerably more sad<br>than angry, once the fire had finally, fully passed.</p><p>I think that's what anger actually does, if you let it —<br>burns through the surface until something truer shows.<br>The rage was never really the deepest, final feeling.<br>It was just the loudest one, the first thing that arose.</p><p>So I let it burn completely, however long it needed.<br>And underneath, I found what genuinely remained —<br>not bitterness, but honest grief, considerably gentler.<br>That's the part, I think, that's actually worth sustained.</p>",
