    body: "<p style='color:#6a4b8f;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:18px'>By Khyati Srivastava</p><p>Marcus had spent years carefully performing constant confidence and fearlessness in front of his genuinely anxious young son, believing that modeling apparent bravery represented good, protective parenting rather than an exhausting performance that, he gradually realized, might actually be teaching his son that genuine fear itself was something shameful to hide rather than something ordinary and survivable.</p><p>Everything shifted during a genuinely difficult period at Marcus's own job, leaving him privately, honestly frightened about the family's financial security in a way he'd carefully hidden from his son throughout weeks of quiet, growing anxiety. His son finally asked directly why he seemed so distracted lately, and Marcus, exhausted by the constant performance, made a deliberate choice to answer honestly for the first time.</p><p>\"I'm genuinely scared about some things at work right now,\" he admitted. \"I don't have it all figured out. I'm working through it, but I'm honestly afraid some days.\" His son, rather than being frightened by the admission, seemed visibly relieved, finally sharing his own persistent anxieties about starting middle school that he'd been carefully hiding, certain his father's apparent fearlessness meant real fear was something he shouldn't admit to feeling himself.</p><p>They talked honestly about fear together for the first time that evening, both genuinely comforted by discovering neither was actually as alone in their anxiety as they'd each assumed. \"I thought I was protecting him by never showing fear,\" Marcus told his own therapist afterward. \"Turns out I was just teaching him that fear itself was something to hide. That wasn't actually the lesson I ever meant to pass along to him at all.\"</p>",
