    created_at: "2026-12-16T16:00:00Z",
    body: "<p style='color:#6a4b8f;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:18px'>By Khyati Srivastava</p><p>I used to wait for change to simply happen to me,<br>some external force reshaping who I'd be.<br>I've learned, instead, that real change takes deliberate choosing —<br>a thousand small decisions, patiently, purposefully.</p><p>I'm choosing patience, even when I'd rather rush.<br>I'm choosing honesty, even when softer lies seem kind.<br>I'm choosing presence over constant, easy distraction.<br>I'm choosing, slowly, the person I actually want to find<br>when I finally look back at who I chose to become.</p><p>It's not dramatic work. It rarely feels significant.<br>Just small, repeated choices, day by ordinary day.<br>But I've learned that's genuinely how real change happens —<br>not all at once, but chosen, deliberately, along the way.</p><p>So I'll keep choosing, slowly, patiently, on purpose.<br>Not waiting for some future version to arrive.<br>I'm building her right now, one honest choice at a time.<br>That's the only way I've found that change actually survives.</p>",
