    created_at: "2026-12-18T16:00:00Z",
    body: "<p style='color:#6a4b8f;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:18px'>By Khyati Srivastava</p><p>I used to think stopping meant I'd genuinely rested —<br>sitting still, phone in hand, body paused but mind still racing on.<br>I called it rest because my body wasn't moving.<br>I never noticed the exhaustion hadn't actually gone.</p><p>Real rest, I've learned, is a considerably different practice —<br>not just stopping motion, but genuinely letting go<br>of the mental noise, the constant inner pressure,<br>the voice insisting I should still be doing something, somehow.</p><p>I had to learn, deliberately, what genuine rest felt like.<br>Not guilty stillness, waiting to get back to work.<br>Actual, full permission to simply, honestly exist<br>without productivity's constant, quiet lurk.</p><p>So here's to learning rest, finally, properly —<br>not the performance of it, but the honest, full thing.<br>It took me considerably longer than it should have.<br>But I've finally found what genuine rest can bring.</p>",
