    body: "<p style='color:#6a4b8f;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:18px'>By Khyati Srivastava</p><p>The Okonkwo family's casual monthly trivia night, begun innocently enough as a simple bonding activity, had escalated over three years into a genuinely elaborate, fiercely competitive event complete with formal team divisions, specialized member roles based on individual expertise — Great-Uncle Bartholomew handling all history questions, teenage cousin Priya covering pop culture and technology — and an increasingly complex, carefully maintained scoring spreadsheet tracking every single monthly competition's results.</p><p>The rivalry reached genuinely absurd new heights when the losing team, three consecutive months running, formally petitioned for a rules committee review, alleging the trivia question difficulty had been unfairly skewed toward the winning team's particular knowledge strengths — a formal grievance that prompted an entire family meeting dedicated specifically to establishing more balanced, mutually agreed-upon question selection protocols going forward.</p><p>The resulting negotiated agreement, complete with a formally documented \"Trivia Night Constitution\" both teams solemnly signed, established rotating question categories and an independent question-selection committee comprised of family members not currently competing in that particular month's match, a bureaucratic solution genuinely disproportionate to the actual stakes involved in what remained, fundamentally, a fun family game night.</p><p>The elaborate system, remarkably, worked exactly as intended, and the family's trivia nights continued for years afterward with genuinely reduced controversy, though several relatives privately admitted they missed the entertaining drama their earlier, less formally regulated competitive disputes had regularly provided.</p>",
