    created_at: "2026-12-19T16:00:00Z",
    body: "<p style='color:#6a4b8f;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:18px'>By Khyati Srivastava</p><p>Everyone celebrates the dramatic kind of strength —<br>surviving the crisis, standing tall through the storm.<br>Nobody writes the songs about the quieter version:<br>simply getting up again in ordinary form.</p><p>The strength it takes to make dinner on a hard day.<br>The strength it takes to answer the phone at all.<br>The strength of showing up to work, genuinely struggling,<br>and still doing your honest job, however small.</p><p>Nobody claps for the unglamorous persistence,<br>the daily choosing to continue, again and again.<br>But I think that quiet strength deserves genuine recognition —<br>not the dramatic rescue, just the steady, patient when<br>you simply keep going, ordinary day after ordinary day.</p><p>So here's to the quiet strength nobody talks about enough,<br>the daily showing up, unglamorous but genuinely true.<br>You don't need a crisis to prove you're genuinely strong.<br>Sometimes strength just looks like simply making it through.</p>",
