    body: "<p style='color:#6a4b8f;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:18px'>By Khyati Srivastava</p><p>The Delgado family's annual holiday cooking competition, judged for the past several years by twelve-year-old Priya as the family's designated impartial youngest member, faced genuine, escalating controversy this particular year when Cousin Marcus formally, seriously disputed her judging methodology after his elaborate soufflé lost to Aunt Rosalind's considerably simpler apple pie.</p><p>Marcus's formal complaint, presented with genuine, entirely disproportionate seriousness at the family's post-dinner gathering, alleged that Priya's scoring criteria unfairly favored traditional, familiar dishes over genuinely ambitious, technically challenging culinary innovation — a grievance that prompted a considerably heated, extended family debate about proper judging standards that consumed nearly an hour of otherwise pleasant holiday gathering time.</p><p>The family ultimately resolved the dispute through an elaborate, formally negotiated compromise: future competitions would feature two separate award categories, \"Most Delicious\" and \"Most Ambitious,\" judged independently to avoid future disputes between traditional comfort food and technically challenging culinary innovation.</p><p>The following year's competition, held under the new dual-category system, produced its own entirely new controversy when several family members argued a single dish should technically qualify for both categories simultaneously — a debate the family resolved, considerably more efficiently this time, by simply awarding it both prizes and moving directly on to dessert. The family's cooking competition remained, everyone agreed, considerably more entertaining for its accumulated bureaucratic absurdity than any simple, uncontested judging process ever could have managed.</p>",
