    created_at: "2026-12-22T16:00:00Z",
    body: "<p style='color:#6a4b8f;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:18px'>By Khyati Srivastava</p><p>I chased a version of success that wasn't mine,<br>borrowed from someone else's definition of complete.<br>Bigger title, bigger house, considerably more of everything.<br>I chased it hard, exhausted, never letting up my feet.</p><p>I finally caught it, genuinely, one exhausted year.<br>And found it hollow, considerably emptier than I'd feared.<br>The success I'd chased was never actually mine to want.<br>It was someone else's dream, borrowed, worn, and wearied.</p><p>I had to build a genuinely honest new definition —<br>not bigger, not more, but considerably more true.<br>Success that fits my actual life, my actual values,<br>the quiet, sustainable kind that carries you<br>through ordinary days without exhausting everything you have.</p><p>So here's to the success I almost missed entirely,<br>chasing someone else's borrowed, hollow dream instead.<br>I've found something considerably smaller, genuinely mine now.<br>Turns out that's the only kind of success that's actually fed.</p>",
