    body: "<p style='color:#6a4b8f;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:18px'>By Khyati Srivastava</p><p>Priya had noticed, over several years, a genuinely strange pattern regarding her car keys: they'd occasionally, inexplicably go missing from their usual, consistent spot on mornings she was genuinely, privately too exhausted, upset, or distracted to safely drive — always reappearing exactly where she'd originally left them once she'd had sufficient time to calm down or rest properly, delaying her departure just long enough to genuinely reconsider whether driving was actually the safest choice that particular morning.</p><p>She began tracking the pattern deliberately after the fourth such coincidence, examining her own genuine emotional and physical state against each missing-key incident, and found the correlation held with a consistency that felt increasingly difficult to attribute to simple, ordinary forgetfulness or carelessness.</p><p>She mentioned the pattern to her sister, half-expecting dismissal, and received instead genuine, thoughtful consideration. \"Maybe you're subconsciously hiding them yourself, somewhere deep down, when you know driving isn't actually safe,\" her sister suggested reasonably. \"Or maybe something else is actually protecting you from getting behind the wheel when you genuinely shouldn't be there.\"</p><p>Priya never found a satisfying answer either way, and eventually stopped feeling frustrated by occasionally missing keys, choosing instead to simply, gratefully treat each occurrence as a genuine signal worth taking seriously rather than dismissing as ordinary carelessness. \"I used to get so annoyed searching for missing keys,\" she told her sister eventually. \"Now I just take it as a sign to actually check in with myself first. It's never once been wrong about when I genuinely wasn't ready to safely drive yet.\"</p>",
