    body: "<p style='color:#6a4b8f;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:18px'>By Khyati Srivastava</p><p>Priya had spent months genuinely hurt and confused by her best friend Naledi's sudden, unexplained emotional withdrawal — cancelled plans, shorter conversations, a general distance that felt, to Priya, like a genuine rejection of a friendship she'd always considered one of her most important relationships.</p><p>She finally, directly confronted the distance during a genuinely difficult conversation, prepared for confirmation that their friendship had simply, quietly run its course. Instead, Naledi broke down, finally, honestly explaining a private struggle she'd been carrying entirely alone: a genuine, serious health scare she'd been processing without telling anyone, including Priya, terrified of burdening her friends with something she hadn't yet fully accepted herself.</p><p>\"I didn't know how to tell you without making it real,\" Naledi admitted, tearful. \"I thought pulling away would somehow protect you from watching me struggle with something I wasn't ready to talk about yet. I didn't realize how much the silence itself would actually hurt you in a completely different way.\"</p><p>Priya understood her friend's distance entirely differently after that admission — not rejection, but genuine, if misguided, protective instinct during a genuinely frightening private struggle. \"I would rather have known and struggled through it together,\" Priya told her gently. \"You don't have to protect me from your hard things. That's genuinely what real friendship is actually for.\" They grew considerably closer afterward, working through Naledi's health struggle together rather than apart, both understanding, finally, exactly what honest vulnerability could offer a genuine friendship that silence never could.</p>",
