    body: "<p style='color:#6a4b8f;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:18px'>By Khyati Srivastava</p><p>Everyone celebrates the courage that gets witnessed —<br>the rescue, the stand taken, the applauded, public brave.<br>Nobody claps for the courage practiced privately,<br>the quiet choice that no one else will ever crave<br>to hear about or celebrate or genuinely notice.</p><p>The courage of asking for help when nobody's forcing you.<br>The courage of finally saying no, unwitnessed, alone.<br>The courage of trying again after a private failure,<br>with nobody watching, nobody needing to be shown.</p><p>I think that private courage might genuinely matter more —<br>the kind that doesn't need an audience to feel complete.<br>You did the brave thing purely because it was right,<br>not because someone was watching you compete<br>for recognition. That's a considerably purer kind of brave.</p><p>So here's to the courage nobody ever witnesses,<br>the private brave choices made when no one's around.<br>That's genuinely the truest test of who you actually are —<br>the courage you practice without needing to be found.</p>",
