    body: "<p style='color:#6a4b8f;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:18px'>By Khyati Srivastava</p><p>Farid brought his grandfather's antique pocket watch to the small repair shop expecting a quick estimate, and found himself instead genuinely fascinated watching Nina work, her careful, methodical process with the watch's tiny gears considerably more absorbing than he'd expected watch repair to be.</p><p>\"You're welcome to just drop it off,\" Nina mentioned after his third visit to check progress, not unkindly. \"Most people don't actually sit and watch the whole repair.\"</p><p>\"Is that a problem?\" he asked, genuinely uncertain whether he'd overstayed some unspoken customer etiquette.</p><p>\"Not remotely,\" she said, a little too quickly, returning her attention to the watch with visible focus that didn't quite hide her smile. The repair, which should have reasonably taken a single afternoon, stretched across nearly two weeks of Farid's regular visits, both of them finding increasingly transparent reasons — a part that needed ordering, a spring that needed \"double-checking\" — to extend a repair that had, by any honest account, actually finished considerably earlier than either admitted.</p><p>The watch worked perfectly when Farid finally took it home. He found reasons to visit the shop anyway, considerably more often than any future watch trouble reasonably justified.</p>",
