    body: "<p style='color:#6a4b8f;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:18px'>By Khyati Srivastava</p><p>Wren hosted the midnight-to-3-a.m. slot at the local radio station, a shift most DJs avoided but she genuinely loved, reading listener-submitted song dedications aloud to an audience of night-shift workers and fellow insomniacs scattered across the city, unable to see any of them but somehow feeling, after years of the same shift, like she knew them anyway.</p><p>One listener's dedications, submitted under the name \"D.\" for over a year, had become genuinely familiar to her — always thoughtful, always slightly melancholy, always signed off with the same small phrase: \"for whoever else is still up.\"</p><p>She started, without fully deciding to, reading a small personal reply into each dedication, an ongoing one-sided conversation with a listener she'd never met and knew almost nothing about beyond a consistent, specific loneliness she recognized in her own late-night shift.</p><p>Dev finally called in directly on a particularly quiet night, nervous enough that Wren could hear it in his voice even before he explained he was the \"D.\" behind a year of dedications. They talked on air for nearly twenty minutes, considerably longer than her usual call-in segments, both surprised at how easily an entire year of one-sided radio correspondence turned into an actual conversation once someone finally picked up the phone.</p>",
