    created_at: "2027-03-29T10:00:00Z",
    body: "<p style='color:#6a4b8f;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:18px'>By Khyati Srivastava</p><p>Cornelius installed the new signal booster on his amateur radio setup mostly to clean up static on frequencies he already used regularly, an unremarkable hardware upgrade he expected to make an unremarkable difference to his ordinary hobby broadcasting.</p><p>He noticed the anomaly within the first week: the booster amplified one specific frequency far more strongly than any of his actual regular channels, a frequency that, according to every official registry he checked, had no assigned broadcaster at all, nobody licensed or unlicensed transmitting on it anywhere in his region.</p><p>He tuned into it out of pure curiosity and found, faintly beneath the amplified static, a repeating structured pattern too regular to be interference, a rhythm his booster kept pulling clearer and clearer with each passing week as though the equipment itself were somehow tuning specifically for it.</p><p>He has stopped mentioning it to other amateur operators after the first few dismissive responses about equipment glitches. He still listens most evenings, the pattern clearer now than it was months ago, still unclaimed by any official source, still arriving faithfully on a frequency that, as far as anyone else can tell, simply doesn't exist.</p>",
