    created_at: "2027-03-30T09:00:00Z",
    body: "<p style='color:#6a4b8f;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:18px'>By Khyati Srivastava</p><p>Ottoline had authenticated antique furniture for over twenty years, developing an instinct for the specific, uneven wear patterns genuine age actually produces, a fluency built from handling thousands of real pieces that no amount of studying could substitute for.</p><p>The heirloom writing desk brought in for appraisal had already passed inspection by two other dealers, its papers, its style, and its general condition all consistent with the two-hundred-year provenance its owner claimed, a seemingly straightforward authentication that should have taken Ottoline twenty minutes.</p><p>Something about the wear on the desk's drawer runners struck her as subtly wrong, worn smooth in a pattern that suggested decades of use, but distributed too evenly, too consistently, lacking the small asymmetries genuine long-term use always leaves behind depending on which hand opened the drawer more often.</p><p>Closer inspection revealed the wear had been deliberately, skillfully applied to new wood rather than accumulated naturally, a sophisticated forgery good enough to fool two experienced appraisers before failing against Ottoline's specific attention to that one detail. \"Anyone can fake old,\" she told the visibly deflated owner, explaining the finding gently. \"It's much harder to fake uneven old. Real wear never distributes itself that evenly. That's usually the tell, if you know exactly where to look.\"</p>",
