    created_at: "2027-03-31T09:00:00Z",
    body: "<p style='color:#6a4b8f;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:18px'>By Khyati Srivastava</p><p>Persimmon the tortoise had roamed the garden center's greenhouse freely for years, mostly ignoring the rotating stock of seedlings entirely, until staff noticed her parking herself, day after day, directly beside one particular struggling tray of sprouts nobody expected to actually take root.</p><p>The seedlings in question had germinated late and weak, already marked for disposal by staff who'd given up on the tray weeks earlier, everything about them suggesting they'd simply never recover enough to sell, an unremarkable batch destined for the compost heap.</p><p>Persimmon, for reasons nobody could explain, positioned her considerable shell to block the tray's worst afternoon sun, shading it for hours at a stretch each day, an accidental greenhouse effect that somehow, gradually, coincided with the weakest sprouts slowly, stubbornly beginning to recover.</p><p>\"We were going to toss that whole tray,\" the greenhouse manager told a customer who'd asked about the unusual scene of a tortoise apparently guarding seedlings. \"She just decided they were worth protecting before we did. I don't know if she actually knew what she was doing, but those sprouts are the healthiest plants in the whole greenhouse now. We're not questioning it.\"</p>",
