    emoji: "💜",
    excerpt: "A couple's habit of claiming the same corner café table for an ongoing jigsaw puzzle becomes a small, cherished tradition the regulars have quietly started protecting for them.",
    created_at: "2027-03-31T10:00:00Z",
    body: "<p style='color:#6a4b8f;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:18px'>By Khyati Srivastava</p><p>Delphine and Ambrose started leaving an unfinished puzzle at their favorite café's corner table nearly two years ago, a small act of trust the owner allowed reluctantly at first, unsure about permanently surrendering a table to two customers' ongoing hobby.</p><p>The puzzle became something of a neighborhood fixture over time, other regulars occasionally adding a piece or two while waiting for their orders, a quiet, collaborative tradition nobody had planned that somehow made the corner table feel less like Delphine and Ambrose's personal property and more like the whole café's shared project.</p><p>The owner, initially hesitant, now actively protects the table from anyone who tries to sit there without understanding its purpose, a small unofficial rule the regular staff enforce without ever being formally asked to.</p><p>\"We could finish it faster if we just did it at home,\" Ambrose admitted once, watching a stranger carefully fit in a single piece before leaving. \"We could,\" Delphine agreed, in no hurry whatsoever. \"But then it'd just be our puzzle. This way it's kind of everyone's. I like that it takes longer. Means we get to keep coming back.\"</p>",
