    created_at: "2027-04-01T09:00:00Z",
    body: "<p style='color:#6a4b8f;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:18px'>By Khyati Srivastava</p><p>Wilhelmina had kept a modest number of hives for eleven years, selling honey at a fair, unchanging price to the same rotating cast of neighborhood families who'd come to depend on her small stand as their regular supply, a business that made her a comfortable, if unspectacular, living.</p><p>A large specialty foods distributor offered to buy her entire annual output at nearly triple her usual price, a deal that would have meant considerably more income for considerably less effort, requiring only that she stop selling directly to her existing neighborhood customers entirely.</p><p>She turned it down within a day of receiving the offer, a decision her accountant found genuinely difficult to understand given the numbers involved, unable to fully explain, even to himself, why the extra income mattered less to her than continuing to sell to families who'd been buying from her stand since her very first harvest.</p><p>\"That distributor's money spends the same as anyone's,\" she told her accountant, declining the offer for good. \"But those families have been buying from me since before I had two working hives. I'm not selling them out to make more money off people who'll never even meet me. Some customers you keep because it's smart business. Some you keep because it's just the right thing to do.\"</p>",
